    "topic_path": "Shakespeare’s plays and poems",
    "content": "William Shakespeare’s literary career includes a diverse range of plays and poems that showcase his mastery of language, storytelling, and character development. His early works, such as Titus Andronicus and Henry VI (Parts 1–3), established his reputation through violent tragedy and historical drama. During the late 16th century, he wrote romantic comedies like A Midsummer Night’s Dream, Much Ado About Nothing, and Twelfth Night, known for their wit and complex characters. His tragedies, including Hamlet, Othello, King Lear, and Macbeth, explore deep psychological and philosophical themes, making them some of the most studied works in literature. Alongside his plays, Shakespeare also composed narrative poems like Venus and Adonis and The Rape of Lucrece, as well as 154 sonnets that delve into love, beauty, and mortality. In his later years, he blended elements of tragedy and comedy in romances like The Tempest and The Winter’s Tale, emphasizing redemption and reconciliation. His works continue to influence literature, theater, and culture worldwide.",
